Agent: You’re on a meaningful path — there are several practical ways to learn more about your great‑aunt’s refugee experience. Below I’ve put together (1) a short workflow you can follow, (2) the most useful archives/databases to check, and (3) extra options (DNA, pros) that often help when records are scarce.

1) Quick research workflow (best order)
- Gather everything you already have: full name (including maiden/alternate spellings), exact or approximate birth date and place, immigration year/port, naturalization info, names of parents/children, any US address, ship name, photos, old letters, or anyone alive who remembers details.
- Talk to family members — even small details (a town name, year, ship route, or religion) can point you to the right record sets.
- Request U.S. records that may exist (see resources below).
- Search passenger/arrival lists and visa files.
- Search refugee/Displaced Persons (DP) camp records and European civil/church records.
- If you hit a wall, consider a professional European/repatriation‑era genealogist.

2) Key U.S. resources to check
- USCIS Genealogy Program — request an A‑file (Alien file) or naturalization file if she immigrated and later naturalized in the U.S. (these files often contain applications, petitions, biographical details and sometimes correspondence). Fees and processing times apply.
- National Archives (NARA) — passenger arrival manifests, immigration/visa files, State Department visa case files (especially useful for mid‑20th century refugees). Search the NARA catalog and contact the National Archives regional office that covers the port of arrival.
- Social Security records (Form SS‑5) — the application for a Social Security number often lists birthplace and parents’ names. You can request a copy via the Social Security Administration (FOIA/genealogy request).
- Passenger lists & ship manifests — accessible via NARA, Ancestry.com, FamilySearch.org, and Fold3. For 1940s arrivals, look for “passenger arrival manifests” (A‑series microfilm at NARA; many digitized on Ancestry/FamilySearch).
- Local county records in the U.S. — naturalization, death certificates, probate and census records (census records will show residence and family members).
- US Holocaust Memorial Museum (USHMM) — if her displacement was related to WWII persecution, the USHMM has collections, testimonies, and tracing resources.
- Arolsen Archives (formerly ITS, Bad Arolsen) — the world’s largest collection on Nazi persecution and displaced persons; they offer searches and can provide documents on DP camp stays and refugee registrations.

3) Key European / German resources
- Arolsen Archives — DP/camp records and tracing service (very relevant for refugees and displaced persons from WWII/afterwards).
- German civil registration (Standesamt) and parish/church records — birth and baptism records (contact the local Standesamt or Landesarchiv for the town named in family lore).
- Local German archives (Stadtarchive / Landesarchive) — may hold residence records or emigration documents.
- If you know the town, search regional archives and genealogical societies (many small towns digitized church books).

4) Contextual resources and background
- NARA/State Dept pages on post‑WWII refugee/immigration programs (Displaced Persons Act, IRO) — useful to understand how refugees were processed.
- Refugee organization histories (International Refugee Organization, IRO; UNRRA) — helps interpret the types of records and terminology you’ll find.
- Online communities: JewishGen, RootsChat, and specialized WWII/displaced persons forums — experienced researchers often provide leads for difficult European searches.

5) Practical tips and expectations
- Spellings vary: try alternate spellings and anglicized versions of German names.
- Records are often slow: official requests (USCIS, NARA, Arolsen) may take weeks–months and may incur fees.
- Protect privacy: for some records you may need to show you are a next‑of‑kin or provide proof of relationship.
- Document everything you find and where you found it — it helps later cross‑checking.

6) If you want help with specific searches
If you want, tell me any of these specifics you already have (exact name(s), approximate birth year, town in Germany if known, year of arrival or port, any US city of settlement). With those I can suggest targeted record sets to check and likely search terms and platforms to use.

